-- 1168: Blog post - "A Portrait Studio in Your Pocket: Inside Our 21 December 2025 Mobile Photography Outdoor Practical"
--
--       Second case-study post for CASL - Master Mobile Photography
--       (TGS-2026064718), from the 21 December 2025 class run taught by
--       Tan Chin Hock - the OUTDOOR PRACTICAL companion to 1167 (the 28 Dec
--       night-photography post, which it cross-links). Four class photographs
--       uploaded to R2 under blog/photography/2025-12-21-* (all verified
--       HTTP 200): group shot by the chrome astronaut sculpture, portrait
--       framed through foliage, phone-on-tripod low angle, portrait against
--       a green backdrop.
--       Ships already published (status 1) and BACK-DATED to the class date
--       2025-12-21; linkedin_urn carries the manual-skip sentinel per the
--       blog-pipeline standing rule: back-dated posts are never auto-shared.
--
-- Course figures identical to 1167, read off the LIVE course page 2026-08-29:
--   full $720 before GST / $784.80 incl / GST $64.80 (9%); nett $424.80
--   (SG/PR 21+, 50%) / $280.80 (MCES/SME, 70%); 2 days / 16 hrs, Beginner,
--   2 hr assessment; Woods Square venue. Funding validity dates deliberately
--   omitted from the body (anachronistic in a Dec-2025-dated post).
--
-- Notes for future editors: same contract as 1165/1167 - ONE-line content,
-- pure-ASCII content with entities, REAL em-dash in excerpt via CHAR(),
-- NO em-dash in meta_description, <details> FAQ, idempotent on url_key,
-- SG-only guard, hero generated on prod post-deploy (kicker: Photography).

SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `linkedin_urn`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'A Portrait Studio in Your Pocket: Inside Our 21 December 2025 Mobile Photography Outdoor Practical', 'mobile-photography-outdoor-practical-singapore-december-2025', CONCAT('On 21 December 2025 our mobile photography class took their phones outdoors with well-known Singapore photographer Tan Chin Hock ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' and turned a walkway, a lawn and a bed of greenery into a portrait studio. Group direction, foreground framing, tripod discipline and subject separation: four habits from one afternoon, all shot on smartphones.'), '<p>The fastest way to improve at photography is not a better camera or a longer lecture &mdash; it is walking out the door with a working professional and shooting real subjects in real light, with someone beside you who can say &quot;move two steps left&quot; at the exact moment it matters.</p> <p>That is what the <strong>21 December 2025</strong> run of our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-master-mobile-photography-capturing-professional-photos-with-your-smartphone.html">CASL - Master Mobile Photography: Capturing Professional Photos with Your Smartphone</a> course did. Led by <strong>Tan Chin Hock</strong>, a well-known Singapore photographer and educator, the class took their phones outdoors for the practical &mdash; and the photographs in this post, all shot on smartphones during that session, show what an afternoon of guided practice produces.</p> <h2>The classroom moves outside</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/photography/2025-12-21-class-group-astronaut.jpg" alt="The 21 December 2025 mobile photography class posing outdoors on a lawn beside a chrome astronaut sculpture, phones in hand" /> <figcaption>The 21 December 2025 class on the outdoor practical, phones up and ready. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Even this group shot is a lesson. It is taken on a phone''s <strong>ultra-wide lens</strong> at arm''s length &mdash; and everything about it is deliberate. The group is arranged in two loose rows on rising ground so every face is visible. The midday sun comes from high behind the camera, lighting faces instead of silhouetting them. And the chrome astronaut sculpture anchors the left of the frame, giving the picture a landmark instead of a plain lawn.</p> <p>Ultra-wide group selfies have two classic failure modes the class learns to manage: edge distortion (put people, not faces, near the frame edges) and the shrinking-heads effect of standing too far back. Get those right and one confident arm''s-length frame beats a dozen &quot;can someone take this for us&quot; attempts.</p> <h2>Lesson 1: Shoot through something</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/photography/2025-12-21-framing-through-foliage.jpg" alt="Candid portrait of a learner photographed through blurred green leaves, shot on a smartphone during the outdoor practical" /> <figcaption>Shooting through foliage: the blurred leaves turn a candid into a composed portrait. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>This candid portrait was not taken from a clear vantage point &mdash; and that is the point. The photographer deliberately shot <strong>through</strong> a plant, letting out-of-focus leaves sweep across the foreground. Because the lens is close to the leaves and focused past them on the subject, the foliage dissolves into soft green shapes that frame the face instead of hiding it.</p> <p>The technique costs one tap: <strong>tap the subject''s face</strong> so the phone locks focus and exposure there, and everything much nearer the lens falls apart into blur. It instantly adds depth, a sense of place, and the feeling that the viewer is glimpsing a real moment &mdash; which is why documentary and wedding photographers lean on it constantly. On the walk, learners practised finding these &quot;peek-through&quot; angles: leaves, railings, doorways, anything that can layer the foreground.</p> <h2>Lesson 2: Stability changes what your phone can do</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/photography/2025-12-21-phone-tripod-low-angle.jpg" alt="A learner crouched behind a smartphone mounted on a small tripod, composing a low-angle shot outdoors" /> <figcaption>Phone on a tripod, photographer at its level: low angle, zero shake, considered frame. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>One learner working exactly the way we teach: phone clamped on a compact tripod, body dropped to the phone''s level, second device in hand for reference. A tripod is usually seen as night-mode equipment, but its bigger gift is <em>discipline</em> &mdash; a fixed frame forces you to compose deliberately, check the edges, and wait for the moment, instead of spraying handheld frames and hoping.</p> <p>The low angle matters too. Phone photos taken from standing eye height all look the same because everyone shoots from there. Drop the camera to waist or knee height and ordinary subjects &mdash; a path, a pet, a child, a puddle reflection &mdash; become graphic and unfamiliar. Add the shutter timer or a volume-button release and nothing touches the phone when the exposure fires.</p> <h2>Lesson 3: Separate your subject from the background</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/photography/2025-12-21-portrait-green-backdrop.jpg" alt="Portrait of a learner holding her smartphone in front of a wall of tall green leaves rendered as soft bokeh" /> <figcaption>A found backdrop: tall greenery, defocused, turns a walkway into a portrait studio. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>The third habit of the afternoon: <strong>choose the background first</strong>. This frame works because the photographer put the subject in front of a bed of tall greenery and let it fall out of focus into an even, saturated wash &mdash; a found studio backdrop, free, three steps off the walkway. The dark sheltered area above reads as a natural vignette.</p> <p>Phones give you two routes to this separation: get physically close with the main lens, or step back and use the telephoto &mdash; both shrink depth of field optically &mdash; and portrait mode can add computational blur when the light or distance will not cooperate. The class covers when each route works and, just as usefully, when portrait mode''s edge detection will fail (wisps of hair, glasses, gaps between fingers) and betray the effect.</p> <h2>What the practical actually builds</h2> <p>Four frames, four transferable habits:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Direct the shot</strong> &mdash; arrange people, mind the sun, use a landmark; a group photo is composed, not collected.</li> <li><strong>Layer the foreground</strong> &mdash; shoot through leaves, rails and frames; tap the subject and let the near layer blur.</li> <li><strong>Stabilise and slow down</strong> &mdash; a tripod or a braced phone plus a considered frame beats twenty hopeful handheld frames.</li> <li><strong>Pick backgrounds deliberately</strong> &mdash; hunt clean, distant, evenly-toned backdrops and let optics or portrait mode melt them.</li> </ol> <p>Back in the classroom, the day rounds out with framing and composition theory, lighting, and editing &mdash; using popular mobile apps to carry a well-captured file to a polished, professional finish.</p> <h2>The course behind the session</h2> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-master-mobile-photography-capturing-professional-photos-with-your-smartphone.html">CASL - Master Mobile Photography: Capturing Professional Photos with Your Smartphone</a> (course code TGS-2026064718) is a <strong>2-day, 16-hour</strong> course at Beginner level with a 2-hour assessment, held at our Woods Square campus, <strong>12 Woodlands Square, Tower 1</strong> &mdash; five minutes'' walk from Woodlands MRT &mdash; and also available as synchronous online training over Zoom.</p> <h3>What it costs</h3> <p>The full fee is <strong>$720</strong> before GST. With CASL funding:</p> <table> <tr><th>Who you are</th><th>Nett fee</th><th>Funding</th></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen / PR, aged 21 and above</td><td><strong>$424.80</strong></td><td>50% course-fee funding</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored</td><td><strong>$280.80</strong></td><td>70% course-fee funding</td></tr> </table> <p>GST of $64.80 (9%) applies to the full course fee. Eligible Singapore Citizens can apply <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong> and <strong>PSEA</strong> against the nett fee, NTUC members may claim <strong>UTAP</strong>, and employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> with <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong> support &mdash; check the course page for the current funding validity window. Completing the course earns a Certificate of Achievement from Tertiary Infotech Academy.</p> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-master-mobile-photography-capturing-professional-photos-with-your-smartphone.html"><strong>Check the next class date and funding eligibility &rarr;</strong></a></p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does the class really shoot outdoors?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. The course pairs classroom theory with practical exercises in real settings &mdash; the photographs in this post all come from the 21 December 2025 outdoor practical. Real light, real subjects and an instructor beside you is where the habits actually form.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need to buy a tripod or accessories?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Every core technique works with just your phone. A compact phone tripod is an inexpensive optional extra that helps with low-angle work, long exposures and self-portraits &mdash; you will see one in action during the class and can decide afterwards whether it suits how you shoot.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">My phone is a few years old. Will the techniques still work?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. Composition, subject separation, foreground framing and stability are optics and judgement, not flagship features. Portrait mode and telephoto lenses extend what is possible, but the lessons that most improve your photographs work on any recent smartphone camera.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is portrait mode cheating?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No &mdash; it is a tool, and the class teaches both the optical routes to background blur (get close, or use the telephoto) and the computational one, including the edge cases where portrait mode''s cut-out fails so you know when to trust it and when to move your feet instead.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is the course funded?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. CASL funding brings the fee down to $424.80 nett for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above, and $280.80 nett for Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES) or SME-sponsored staff. SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA, UTAP, SFEC and Absentee Payroll support apply &mdash; see the course page for details and the current validity window.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>Every frame above came out of the camera its photographer already owned. What changed in one afternoon was the seeing &mdash; and that is trainable.</p> <ol> <li>This week, shoot one portrait through a foreground layer &mdash; leaves, a railing, a doorway. Tap the face, let the layer blur, compare it with a straight-on version.</li> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-master-mobile-photography-capturing-professional-photos-with-your-smartphone.html"><strong>CASL - Master Mobile Photography</strong></a> run with Tan Chin Hock &mdash; from <strong>$280.80 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above.</li> <li>Read the companion post from the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/mobile-photography-class-singapore-december-2025">28 December 2025 run</a> &mdash; five night-photography lessons, from leading lines to shooting craters on the moon.</li> </ol>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2025-12-21', 'TGS-2026064718', 'TGS-2026064718', 'Smartphone Portrait Photography Class Singapore | CASL Course', 'Four smartphone portrait lessons from our 21 December 2025 outdoor practical with photographer Tan Chin Hock: directing group shots, framing through foliage, tripod discipline and subject separation. CASL-funded course from $280.80 nett.', 'smartphone portrait photography, mobile photography class singapore, phone photography course, portrait mode tips, foreground framing, phone tripod, casl photography course, skillsfuture photography, tan chin hock photographer, outdoor photography practical', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'mobile-photography-outdoor-practical-singapore-december-2025') x);

